  "ptDouleur": "Pain or cracked nipples|Check the latch: mouth wide open, a large part of the areola in the mouth.|Start with the less painful breast.|After the feed, spread a little milk on the nipple and let it air-dry.|If the pain lasts more than a few days, talk to a health worker.",
  "ptEngorgement": "Hard, swollen breasts|Breastfeed often, day and night, without limiting the time.|Before the feed, express a little milk by hand to soften the breast.|After the feed, a cool cloth eases the swelling.|Red, hot, painful breast with fever: keep breastfeeding and get care quickly.",
  "ptPeuDeLait": "Afraid of not having enough milk|The more baby feeds, the more milk you make: offer the breast at least 8 times in 24 hours.|Keep baby skin to skin and offer both breasts at each feed.|Baby gets enough milk if they wet at least 6 nappies a day and gain weight.|Give no water nor other milk: it reduces your milk.",
  "ptPriseDuSein": "Baby latches badly or falls asleep|Wait until the mouth is wide open, then bring baby quickly to the breast.|The chin touches the breast, the nose stays free.|If baby falls asleep, switch breasts or stroke the cheek.|If the problem goes on, ask a health worker for help.",
  "ptRepriseTravail": "Away from baby (work, travel)|Express your milk by hand into a clean, covered container.|Expressed milk keeps up to 4 hours at room temperature, and up to 4 days in the fridge.|Give it by cup or spoon.|Breastfeed as soon as you are with baby, and at night.",
  "ptBackToExclusive": "Back to exclusive breastfeeding|Until 6 months, breast milk is enough, even when it is hot: no water, no herbal tea.|Offer the breast more often to make more milk.|To stop another milk, get help from a health worker.",
  "ptStageFirstWeek": "The first days|The first milk (colostrum), thick and yellow, protects baby: give it.|Breastfeed 8 to 12 times in 24 hours, day and night.|Keep baby skin to skin against you as much as possible.",
  "ptStageFirstWeeks": "The first weeks|Breastfeed on demand, as soon as baby looks for the breast or puts hands to the mouth.|Around 3 and 6 weeks, baby may feed much more for a few days: it is normal, your milk adapts.|Let baby finish one breast before offering the other.",
  "ptStageSettled": "Baby is growing|Keep feeding on demand, at night too.|Feeds may get shorter: baby feeds faster.|Breast milk alone is enough until 6 months.",
  "ptStageBeforeFoods": "Soon 6 months|Keep breast milk alone until 6 months: no water nor other food before.|At 6 months, you will start other foods while breastfeeding.|If you go back to work, learn to express your milk by hand.",
  "ptStageWithFoods": "After 6 months|Keep breastfeeding until 2 years or more.|Breastfeed before or after meals, as suits baby.|Offer varied foods several times a day.",
